 ## Overview
-SVDAG Parameter Configurations define the specific bit-widths for Child Node Masks (CHNM), Child Node Pointers (PT), and Addressing Words (addr. word) within a Symmetry-aware Sparse Voxel DAG (SSVDAG) node layout [S1]. They solve the problem of optimizing memory footprint versus addressable space in hierarchical data structures by allowing variable-length pointers [S1]. Unlike the base SVO configuration which uses fixed 32-bit pointers, these configurations (SVDAG1–6) enable the use of shorter pointers (8-bit or 16-bit) for nodes located closer to the root, significantly reducing memory consumption for large scenes while maintaining the ability to address distant nodes via longer pointers [S1].
+SVDAG Parameter Configurations define the specific bit-widths for Child Node Masks (CHNM), Child Node Pointers (PT), and Addressing Words within a Symmetry-aware Sparse Voxel DAG (SSVDAG) node layout [S1]. They optimize the trade-off between memory footprint and addressable space in hierarchical data structures by allowing variable-length pointers [S1]. Unlike the base SVO configuration, which uses a single fixed 32-bit pointer type, configurations SVDAG2–SVDAG6 introduce multiple pointer types (PT1, PT2, PT3) with lengths of 8, 16, or 32 bits to reduce memory consumption for large scenes while maintaining addressability [S1]. SVDAG1 remains identical to the base SVO configuration [S1].
 
 ## How it works
